Planar long-range dispersion examples
=====================================

Planar Lennard-Jones liquid-vapor slab (1267 atoms, fully periodic, with
the inhomogeneous direction along z) using the "planar" long-range
dispersion solvers for the 1/r^6 van der Waals interaction.

These styles are designed for systems whose mean density varies in only
one direction (e.g. a planar liquid-vapor interface).  Instead of the
full 3-D reciprocal sum of ewald/disp or pppm/disp, they evaluate a 1-D
Fourier sum (ewald) or 1-D FFT (pppm) over the z wavevectors of the
dispersion-weighted density and treat the two homogeneous lateral
directions analytically, which is much cheaper for planar systems.

Three new styles are demonstrated:

  pair_style lj/cut/dispplanar <rcut> <Delta>
      Matched short-range pair style.  Evaluates the full LJ to
      rcut+Delta and supplies the real-space shell correction.  The
      1/r^6 dispersion is split at rcut by a C3 (septic) smoothstep over
      the shell [rcut, rcut+Delta].  Incompatible with
      "pair_modify tail yes" (the kspace handles the tail).

  kspace_style ewald/disp/planar <accuracy>
      1-D z Fourier-sum Ewald solver for the long-range 1/r^6 tail.

  kspace_style pppm/disp/planar <accuracy>
      Mesh-accelerated (1-D FFT, O(N)) version of ewald/disp/planar.
      A Kokkos variant pppm/disp/planar/kk is also available.

Input scripts:

  in.planar_nve     pair lj/cut/dispplanar + kspace ewald/disp/planar,
                    NVE run demonstrating energy conservation.

  in.planar_gamma   pair lj/cut/dispplanar + kspace pppm/disp/planar,
                    NVT run computing the long-range surface tension
                    gamma = 0.5*Lz*(Pzz - 0.5*(Pxx+Pyy)).

These styles require fully periodic boundaries (p p p), a 3-D orthogonal
(non-triclinic) box, and must NOT be combined with the EW3DC
"kspace_modify slab" correction.  Several kspace_modify keywords (kmax,
corr, contour, pressure/profile, dim) tune their behavior; see the
kspace_modify doc page.

data.lj_slab is a 1267-atom LJ liquid-vapor slab configuration.
